  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAs of 230 AM Sunday, a highly amplified midlevel ridge across the \ncentral US is flanked by troughs on each coast. A complex of \nthunderstorms continues to move across portions of western NE and \nwithin a broad zone of 850-700 mb WAA. As the LLJ veers near dawn \nthis morning, a few thunderstorms cannot be ruled out across central \nKS. Later this afternoon, a weak low-level baroclinic zone across \neastern KS will overlap with a very moist airmass comprised of PW \nvalues up to 2\". By mid-afternoon up to 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE will \ndevelop across eastern KS (generally east of I-135). This is likely \nto yield at least isolated thunderstorms. Similar to Saturday, \neffective shear will remain 20-25 kt, supporting mainly pulse \nconvection. DCAPE up to 1300 J/kg may support localized downbursts \nnear 60 mph. A similar scenario may play out Monday afternoon across \neastern KS. In regards to temperatures, with the ridge axis \noverhead, afternoon highs near 100 degrees are expected area-wide \nthrough Tuesday. The Heat Advisory remains in effect through Tuesday \nevening. \n\nMid to long range guidance are trending towards an end of the long \nstretch of unseasonably hot temperatures. The initial pattern change \nbegins late Tuesday into Wednesday as a midlevel shortwave trough \nejects across the northern Plains, leaving zonal midlevel flow in \nits wake. In addition, a surface cold front will slowly move \nacross the region Wednesday into Thursday. This frontal passage \nshould yield increasing rain chances and high temperatures \ncloser to normal in the low 90s. A secondary surface trough will\ndevelop across the high Plains late Thursday into Friday ahead \nof a stronger midlevel shortwave trough. With the return of \nsoutherly low-level flow, temperatures will return to the mid \nand upper 90s Friday and Saturday. The aforementioned shortwave\ntrough is progged to eject into the northern Plains late \nSaturday, shunting a strong cold front through the area late \nSaturday night into Sunday. This frontal passage may be \naccompanied by widespread rainfall as well. Stay tuned.",
